            <ArticleTitle>[Postpartum left ovarian vein thrombosis: case report and literature review].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>499-504</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Ovarian Vein Thrombosis is an uncommon complication of the puerperium. Its unspecific clinical presentation and its low incidence make it difficult to diagnose.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 36-year-old pregnant woman has a vaginal delivery at 39+2 weeks of gestation and develops left lumbar pain irradiated to the left iliac fossa. She is diagnosed of left ovarian vein thrombosis by sonography and TC and receives anticoagulant treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A high index of suspicion is the key to a correct diagnosis and treatment. Confirmation with modern imaging methods is crucial for the diagnosis and treatment nowadays.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The best results and lower rate of complications are achieved with an early diagnosis and anticoagulant treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
